# Vision

Computer vision service for hit detection: given a frame, detect people and
their segmentation masks, then test whether a crosshair shape intersects any
of them. See [docs/services/vision.md](../../docs/services/vision.md) for
the service's role, boundaries, and inputs/outputs in the wider pipeline.

## Setup

Requires Python 3.13+ and [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/).

```bash
cd apps/vision
uv sync
```

Model weights (`*.pt`) are not committed to git (see root `.gitignore`).
`yolov8n.pt` / `yolov8n-seg.pt` are pulled automatically by `ultralytics` on
first use; `best.pt` is a fine-tuned checkpoint produced by
[`src/snapshoot_train.ipynb`](#training-notebook) and must be placed in this
directory manually if you want to use it in place of the stock model.

## Usage

`src/detector.py` exposes the `Detector` class (`process()` for
detection/segmentation, `is_hit()` for crosshair intersection) and doubles
as a CLI for trying it out against a single image:

```bash
uv run src/detector.py --image assets/example.jpg
```

Useful flags:

| Flag                 | Default                | Description                                                             |
| -------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `-i, --image`         | `assets/example.jpg`    | Image to run detection on.                                               |
| `--od-enabled`        | off                      | Run object detection first, then segment each crop, instead of segmenting the whole frame directly. |
| `--od-model`           | `yolov8n.pt`             | Object detection weights (only used with `--od-enabled`).                |
| `--seg-model`          | `yolov8n-seg.pt`         | Segmentation weights.                                                    |
| `--radius`             | `10`                     | Radius (px) of the example crosshair hit circle at the image center.     |
| `-o, --output`         | none                     | Save the debug overlay (detections + hit circle) to this path.           |
| `--no-display`         | off                      | Don't open a GUI preview window — use for headless/SSH/CI runs.          |

Run `uv run src/detector.py --help` for the full list.

## Training notebook

[`src/snapshoot_train.ipynb`](src/snapshoot_train.ipynb) is a Colab notebook
that produces a fine-tuned segmentation checkpoint. It's exploratory/offline
tooling, not something CI runs — steps:

1. Pulls the [`sebnae/IndoorCrowd`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/sebnae/IndoorCrowd)
   dataset from the Hugging Face Hub (needs an `HF_SECRET` token set in the
   Colab environment).
2. Converts its RLE segmentation masks to YOLO-format polygon labels and
   lays out a `yolo_dataset/` directory (`images/`, `labels/`,
   `data.yaml`) split into train/val/test.
3. Fine-tunes a `yolov8n-seg` checkpoint on that dataset via
   `ultralytics.YOLO(...).train(...)`.
4. Optionally uploads the resulting run directory back to the Hugging Face
   Hub.

The resulting checkpoint is what `best.pt` is expected to be — copy it into
`apps/vision/` and pass `--seg-model best.pt` (or set
`seg_model_path="best.pt"` when constructing `Detector`) to use it.

## Tests

```bash
uv run pytest
```

`tests/test_vision.py` runs the real segmentation model against
`assets/example.jpg`, checking `Detector.process()`'s output shape and
`Detector.is_hit()`'s intersection math. See
[Testing Strategy](../../docs/development/testing.md) for how this fits into
the repo's broader test layout, and `.github/workflows/ci.yaml` for how it's
run automatically.
